WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.360
In high school, the days tend to blend together&nbsp;
after a while. Math test, unrequited love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:11.040
annoying group project, stress, rinse, repeat.&nbsp;
Sometimes a big football game or a school dance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.760
or maybe a fight in the hallway&nbsp;
breaks up the monotony a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.200
but it’s normally pretty uneventful.
That’s always been fine by me. I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.440
never tried to be a popular kid or the center&nbsp;
of attention, I prefer to keep my head down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.280
work stage crew for the school plays, and just&nbsp;
hang out with the same small group of friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.120
I’ve known since we were little kids.
It was on one of these ordinary days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.720
in a long line of similarly ordinary days, that&nbsp;
something came to destroy the peaceful high school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.160
existence I’d gotten used to. As I sat in history&nbsp;
class, passing notes with my best friend Zach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.560
and ignoring a video about the Civil War, I&nbsp;
had no idea that I was about to live through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.820
the worst day of my life. And I would be one of&nbsp;
the lucky ones; I’d survive. The others…let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.800
just say they wouldn’t be so lucky.
I was just scribbling a response to Zach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.320
something about hanging out and playing&nbsp;
Xbox after school, when the PA system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.760
crackled to life. Our principal’s voice&nbsp;
cut through the droning narration about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.600
the Battle of Antietam as he spoke.
“Staff, this is a lockdown. Initiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.280
your lockdown procedures immediately. If you&nbsp;
are a visitor outside the building, please&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.160
leave the property immediately, and call 911.”
I assumed it was just another lockdown drill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.360
until I saw my teacher’s face. She looked pale,&nbsp;
her expression tight, like she was trying to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.520
it together for our benefit. I could see her hands&nbsp;
shaking as she reached to turn off the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.360
and when I heard the tremor in her voice I knew&nbsp;
for sure: she was terrified. This was not a drill.&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.480
As our teacher locked the door and we all&nbsp;
climbed under our desks to crouch in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.120
dark and wait out the lockdown, I could hear&nbsp;
my classmates whispering feverishly. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.720
was trying to figure out what was going on.&nbsp;
Someone suggested a kid had brought a knife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.720
to school. Someone wondered aloud if a threat&nbsp;
had been called in to the principal’s office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.120
Zach cracked a dumb joke about a mutated frog&nbsp;
escaping from the biology lab, when suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.160
a scream rang out, echoing down the hall.
Have you ever felt an entire room of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.400
holding their breath at once? I have.
We listened for the sound of fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.440
for gunshots, anything
...Nothing. And then another scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.880
louder, closer, and longer than the last.&nbsp;
It was a scream of pure, unadulterated fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.920
and pain that dissolved into a wet gurgle that&nbsp;
made my stomach turn. Zach and I shared a look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.240
one that said, “whatever is going on,&nbsp;
it’s like nothing we’ve ever seen before.”&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.240
I could feel my chest tightening, and it was&nbsp;
getting harder to breathe. I tucked my head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.600
between my knees, holding onto the back of my head&nbsp;
and trying to take in deep gulps of air. The room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.240
felt as if it tilted on its side and I thought&nbsp;
I might pass out, when another sound brought me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:38.240
back to earth. It was coming from the back wall&nbsp;
of the classroom, by the chalkboard. The sound of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.680
liquid sloshing onto the floor, mixed with plaster&nbsp;
splintering apart and pieces clattering all over.&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.480
I tried to get a better look, but the&nbsp;
room was dark and I didn’t want to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.000
the relative safety of my desk. If there was&nbsp;
someone, or something, coming into the room…I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.880
didn’t want it to see me.
Zach had a better angle than me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.240
I could see him craning his neck to get&nbsp;
a better look, when he clapped a hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.960
over his mouth in shock. I heard him scream,&nbsp;
muffled, against his hand. What had he seen?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.600
What was it? Before I could even ask, my teacher’s&nbsp;
cry ripped through the darkness and the silence.&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.400
“Get out! Get out!” She repeated over and over&nbsp;
again, and I could hear the raw desperation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.360
her voice. It was like someone ushering passengers&nbsp;
off of the sinking Titanic, someone who knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.000
there is no hope for them but hopes they can at&nbsp;
least save the children before it all goes down.&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.760
“Run! For the love of God just run!” Her voice&nbsp;
broke, from fear, from pain, or both, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.400
sound was enough to get my legs working again. My&nbsp;
classmates had the same response, and we blindly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.400
rushed for the door, flipping over desks and&nbsp;
knocking chairs to the side as we scrambled for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.440
exit. Zach grabbed my arm, and I held on tight,&nbsp;
afraid I would lose him in the chaos. Someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.960
unlocked the door and we poured out into the hall.
Just as I made it out of the door, I couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:50.320
help but turn back and look. Call it morbid&nbsp;
curiosity, call it instinct, call it stupidity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.040
but I had to see what had happened. I turned&nbsp;
just in time to see a nightmare brought to life.&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:02.240
The wall was melting, covered in something thick,&nbsp;
black, and putrid. The smell invaded my nostrils&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.120
and I gagged on it. A wretched, grey arm had&nbsp;
its fingers wrapped around the teacher’s wrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.360
skin peeling off to reveal muscle and bone.&nbsp;
It looked like it belonged to a corpse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.920
but it gripped her with inhuman strength. She&nbsp;
was pulling with all her might, but I watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.040
helplessly as the arm pulled her into the melted&nbsp;
wall like she weighed nothing, swallowing her body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.600
and her screams with it. And then she was gone.
Zach tugged on my arm, hard, yanking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.680
me into the hall.
“Come on man, run!”&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.720
He hadn’t seen what I’d seen, or&nbsp;
he would have realized that running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.200
might not be enough. Whatever this thing&nbsp;
was, it didn’t play by any rules I knew.&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.200
Running was all we had right now, though,&nbsp;
so we ran. I looked around for a weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.600
something, anything I could use to defend myself,&nbsp;
but I couldn’t see anything that would help.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.480
Instead, all I saw was the destruction the&nbsp;
intruder had left in its wake. All around me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.600
the halls I walked through every day were falling&nbsp;
to pieces. They weren’t just damaged. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.040
coming apart at the seams. Lockers were warped&nbsp;
beyond recognition, walls completely dissolved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.040
sections of the floor reduced to&nbsp;
sizzling puddles of goo, and everywhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.720
everything was covered in that same black slime.
It smelled worse than you can ever imagine. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.920
smelled like the complete loss of hope, like&nbsp;
the point of no return. It smelled like death.&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.760
We reached the end of the hall when I heard&nbsp;
something shifting behind us. I suddenly became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.200
aware that we were alone. The sounds of frantic&nbsp;
footsteps, hushed whispers, doors slamming shut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:28.480
and locking, had all quieted. Where was everyone?
I glanced over my shoulder, and saw one lone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.880
figure shambling down the hall toward us. It was&nbsp;
far away, and I could scarcely make it out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.480
darkness, but I could hear dripping, sizzling,&nbsp;
and the smell of decay grew stronger. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.760
not a classmate or a teacher. Whatever it was, it&nbsp;
was taking people away, or killing them, I didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.800
know exactly what. But whatever it was doing&nbsp;
to everyone, Zach and I were next on its list.&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.480
The scariest part was how slowly it moved. Maybe&nbsp;
its legs were too rotten to move any faster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.520
joints disintegrating into useless mush,&nbsp;
but it seemed more deliberate than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.440
To me, in my panic-clouded mind, it&nbsp;
seemed that it was taking its time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.880
It didn’t need to rush, it knew that, when it&nbsp;
wanted to catch its prey, it would. I couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.600
suppress the sick feeling in my stomach this&nbsp;
time, I keeled over and vomited onto the floor.&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.120
When I looked up, Zach was beckoning to me&nbsp;
from just inside the doors to the auditorium.&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.360
“Come on! In here!”
He could have left me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.400
behind for the intruder to take, tried to save&nbsp;
himself, and sacrifice the slow friend frozen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.680
place with terror, but here he was looking out&nbsp;
for me again. Even in this horrible situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.760
I felt a surge of gratitude. I booked it&nbsp;
across the floor, forgetting for a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.640
about the shuffling corpse making its way toward&nbsp;
us, and joined my best friend in the auditorium.&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.040
It was eerie in the dark, the curtain&nbsp;
soaking up and dampening sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.120
barely any light illuminating the normally&nbsp;
cheerful props and backdrops left from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.800
recent production of Alice in Wonderland. A once&nbsp;
friendly place felt twisted, like a mockery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.680
Wonderland with a monster stalking outside&nbsp;
and waiting to pounce. We sat on the stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.480
listening to the sound of our labored breathing.&nbsp;
The air felt heavy, but I welcomed the smell of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.080
paint and dust over the nauseating stench&nbsp;
that permeated the rest of the school.&nbsp;

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.680
“Are we the only ones left?” Zach&nbsp;
whispered, breaking the silence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.680
He was the first one to say what we&nbsp;
were both thinking, what we both feared.&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.280
“No.” I said with false confidence. “I think&nbsp;
some of them got out, before…” I couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.080
finish the sentence. “Somebody had to get out.&nbsp;
It can’t just be us.” I leaned my head against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.960
a giant papier-mache toadstool, glad it was too&nbsp;
dark for Zach to see the tears filling my eyes.&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.360
“What was that thing?” He asked.
“Did you see it?” I sat up straighter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.800
I thought I was the only one who’d gotten&nbsp;
a look at the thing, however fleeting.&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.880
“No. But I heard it. And…smelled it.” He coughed.
It took me a while to answer his question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:50.640
Eventually, I found the word. “It’s evil.”
We sat in silence for a long while after that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:57.120
I don’t know exactly how long. Moments like that,&nbsp;
where at any second you could be lost forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.240
time stretches out into something unrecognizable.&nbsp;
I was starting to think, foolishly, that we might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:08.560
be in the clear, when I heard a strange sound.&nbsp;
It was a creaking, like footsteps on an old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.560
wooden floor. I looked around for the source&nbsp;
of the noise, but I couldn’t see anything.&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:19.920
It sounded close, impossibly close.&nbsp;
Creak. Creak. One step then another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.320
Where was that coming from? There was no&nbsp;
wood floor in the hall, nothing above us.&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.040
By the time I understood, it was too late.&nbsp;
The top of a head, slimy, clammy and grey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.600
with tufts of limp white hair clinging to&nbsp;
the soft flesh began to emerge from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.960
stage where we sat. Eyes, hollow and dead,&nbsp;
but somehow alert and searching for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:44.800
came next, followed by a gaping wound where&nbsp;
a nose should be, and a wide, toothless grin.&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:51.040
It looked almost like a person, but it was wrong.&nbsp;
Just wrong. Every cell in my body rejected it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.680
screaming at me with thousands of years of animal&nbsp;
instinct that I had to run. I grabbed Zach’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:59.600
arm and yanked, but he didn’t come with me.
I turned to look, and my heart jumped into my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:05.680
throat. It had him. Its bony fingers had a hold&nbsp;
on his legs, and he was already halfway gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:11.040
pulled into a slick black puddle in the stage.&nbsp;
His face was damp with tears, as the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:16.480
of his fate set in. He grabbed my hand in his,&nbsp;
and squeezed. “Run.” Was all he could get out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:21.600
before the old man pulled him out of sight.
So I obeyed my best friend’s wish. I ran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.000
and I hid in the prop closet, hand over&nbsp;
my mouth to stifle my breathing. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.240
there was a loud bang, and I heard voices&nbsp;
yelling. Something that sounded like a gunshot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.120
A blast of bright light. Then, a voice, male&nbsp;
and official-sounding, called out: “Is anyone in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:39.360
here? You can come out, we’re with the police.”
I opened the closet door and peered out, to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.760
people in hazmat suits wielding bizarre weapons&nbsp;
and carrying devices I’d never seen before.&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.000
The man who had spoken approached me, took&nbsp;
my hand, and asked me if I’d seen anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.720
I got the strong sense that I should definitely&nbsp;
not tell him what I’d seen. “No, sir,” I said.&nbsp;

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.960
He nodded. Whether he believed me or not,&nbsp;
he knew I wouldn’t say anything more.&nbsp;

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.320
“Gas leak.” He said. “It destroyed&nbsp;
large parts of the building. Highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:06.400
dangerous. We need to get you out of here.”
They took me home and I hugged my parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.040
tighter than I ever had before. That night,&nbsp;
the news covered the tragedy at my school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.880
The “gas leak” had taken dozens of lives&nbsp;
before the authorities had gotten there.&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:21.360
Everyone else can accept that story all they want.&nbsp;
I know the truth. I see it when I close my eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:27.120
imagine it standing over me when I wake up&nbsp;
in the dead of night. Those hollow eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:35.360
that horrible smile, the stench of death and rot.&nbsp;
It looked right at me. It saw me. It wanted…I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:40.240
can’t let myself think about what it wanted.
I don’t know exactly what I saw, but I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:45.760
a few things for certain: There was no gas&nbsp;
leak. Zach and the rest of the people it took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:50.800
are gone. And, if that monster ever&nbsp;
escapes…it’s coming after me next.&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.480
Now go check out “SCP-106 - The Old&nbsp;
Man Escape” and “SCP-106 - The Old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:03.760
Man Origin Theories” for more explorations&nbsp;
into the nightmare factory that is SCP-106!

